<http://idearq.org/resource/article/959915> a ns1:AcademicArticle ;
    dcterms:creator <http://idearq.org/resource/author/c_bronk_ramsey>,
        <http://idearq.org/resource/author/g_j_van_klinken>,
        <http://idearq.org/resource/author/r_a_housley>,
        <http://idearq.org/resource/author/r_e_m_hedges> ;
    dcterms:identifier "959915" ;
    dcterms:issued "1993"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dcterms:title "Radiocarbon Dates from the Oxford AMS System: Archaeometry Datelist 17" ;
    ns1:abstract "Comment (R. M. J.): the Pleistocene fauna from Steetley Wood Cave differs from any so far reported from Creswell Crags, less than 5 km to the south-west, in its limited range of speciesbear, wolf, fox, reindeer and a bovine (seemingly bison).In this it resembles the much larger faunal sample from Windy Knoll near Castleton, Derbyshire.The infinite age obtained offers no contradiction to the hypothesis that these and other equivalent assemblages pre-date faunas rich in horse, woolly rhinoceros, mammoth and spotted hyaena as are well documented at Creswell.Such a sequence appears to exist at Picken's Hole, Somerset (Tratmdn, 1964).There is no evidence for a human presence at Steetley Wood Cave, nor can such a presence be demonstrated for any of the other localities with a similar fauna. Little HoyleSamples of animal tooth and bone (collection of BM(NH)) from Little Hoyle Cave, Dyfed (NGR SS 11 1999) submitted" ;
    ns1:doi "10.1111/j.1475-4754.1993.tb01046.x" .

